"""32 original paired texts; 64 distinct comparison and discussion tasks."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
pairs=[]
def pair(a,b,ask,correct,wrongs,why,discuss,note):pairs.append((a,b,ask,correct,wrongs,why,discuss,note))
pair('Children: Bring your hat and water bottle for our walk.','Families: Please help your child pack a named hat and water bottle for the walk.','How do both messages support the same purpose?','They help prepare children for the walk.', ['They both tell an imaginary walking adventure.','They both ask families to attend the walk.','They both explain how hats are made.'],'Both concern preparing the same items, but they address the child doing the packing and the adult helping.','Explain why “help your child” suits the second message but would not suit a message to the children.','Identify the adult helper role; children are the ones being helped. Keep the preparation purpose in view.')
pair('New players: Move one space each turn. A turn is your chance to move.','Returning players: This version still uses one move per turn.','What extra support does the first text provide?','It explains a basic term for someone new.', ['It gives returning players an extra turn.','It changes the number of spaces moved.','It asks readers to buy the game.'],'Both explain play, but only the beginner version defines turn.','Discuss why a writer may keep an explanation short for readers who already know the game.','Link the different amount of explanation to prior knowledge; do not claim every returning player knows every rule.')
pair('Young readers: This beetle has six legs. Count three on each side.','Older readers: The diagram labels the beetle’s six legs in two groups of three.','Which idea is kept in both texts?','The beetle has six legs arranged in two groups of three.', ['The beetle has three legs altogether.','The beetle is a story character who can count.','The diagram tells readers to buy a beetle.'],'The same information is expressed through a direct counting invitation or a diagram description.','Compare how the counting invitation and the diagram description could help their different readers.','Mention direct participation for young readers and labelled representation for older readers; accept other text-supported observations.')
pair('Dear Principal, please add a shaded seat near the oval so students can rest comfortably.','Classmates, support our idea for a shaded seat! It would give us a comfortable rest spot.','Why does the writer use a request in A and an invitation to support in B?','The principal can consider the change; classmates can support the proposal.', ['Only classmates are allowed to sit down.','The principal is being asked to tell a bedtime story.','B asks for the seat to be removed.'],'Both persuade towards the same improvement but ask different audiences for different kinds of action.','Explain one shared benefit and one audience-specific action in these messages.','Shared benefit: comfortable shaded rest. Different actions: consider/add the seat versus support the idea.')
pair('Little listeners: A tiny mouse wore a huge hat. Down it slipped—right over his eyes!','Independent readers: The mouse’s oversized hat slid over his eyes just as he stepped onto the stage.','What shared purpose can both extracts have?','Entertaining readers with the mouse’s hat problem.', ['Explaining how real hats are manufactured.','Giving a factual count of wild mice.','Persuading a shop to sell only small hats.'],'Both tell an invented event; sentence detail and vocabulary differ.','Compare a language choice that makes each version suitable for its named readers.','Accept short simple sentences and an exclamation in A, or oversized and the stage setting adding detail in B. No requirement to prefer either version.')
pair('Visitors: Follow the blue arrows to the hall.','Staff setting up: Put the blue arrows along the path to guide visitors to the hall.','What changes between these messages?','The readers’ roles in using or placing the directions.', ['The destination changes from hall to library.','The arrows change from blue to red.','Both readers are asked to remove the arrows.'],'The shared aim is helping visitors reach the hall; staff arrange the signs and visitors follow them.','Discuss why swapping these two messages could confuse their readers.','Visitors need directions, not a setup job; staff need to know where to place signs. Keep the common destination accurate.')
pair('Beginner gardeners: Push the seed gently into the soil and cover it.','Experienced helpers: Demonstrate seed placement and covering for each beginner.','How are the texts connected?','Both support seed planting, but one gives the learner steps and the other guides a helper.', ['They describe two unrelated sports.','Both ask beginners to teach the helpers.','They give opposite instructions about covering seeds.'],'The activity stays planting while the audience’s responsibility changes.','Explain how the helper’s demonstration could make the beginner’s instruction easier to follow.','Link showing seed placement/covering to the stated steps; no unprovided growing rule is needed.')
pair('Families: The play begins at 2 pm. Please be seated before the curtain opens.','Performers: Be ready backstage before the 2 pm opening.','Which information must remain consistent across both versions?','The performance begins at 2 pm.', ['Everyone must wait backstage.','Everyone must sit in the audience.','The performance is cancelled.'],'The roles and locations differ but both depend on the same opening time.','Describe how the locations are suited to the two audiences while the purpose stays event preparation.','Families go to seats; performers go backstage. Both prepare for the same opening.')
pair('Younger readers: A map is a drawing that helps you find places.','Map-club members: Use the map key to interpret the symbols before comparing routes.','Why is the first text simpler?','It introduces the basic idea of a map.', ['It tells every reader that map keys are useless.','It requires more map knowledge than the second.','It gives the exact shortest route.'],'Both help people understand maps, but A starts with a definition while B assumes that basic concept.','Explain what knowledge the second writer seems to expect the readers already have.','They know what a map is and are ready to use a key and compare routes; do not infer their exact ages.')
pair('Classmates: Join our lunchtime drawing group and share ideas for new pictures.','Families: Encourage your child to try our lunchtime drawing group if they enjoy making pictures.','Which shared aim links the two invitations?','Encouraging participation in the drawing group.', ['Selling finished pictures to every family.','Reporting how many drawings were made yesterday.','Asking families to join a lunchtime race.'],'Both promote participation, directed either to potential child members or adults supporting them.','Compare the action requested from children with the action requested from families.','Children join/share; families encourage their child. Do not say families are required to attend.')
pair('First-time users: Press the green button to begin recording your voice.','Trained helpers: Check that learners can find the green record button before they start.','Why does the second text say “check” rather than simply “press”?','It addresses someone supporting another user.', ['It changes the button from green to blue.','It asks the helper to delete all recordings.','It is telling a story about a talking button.'],'The shared purpose is beginning a recording successfully; the helper’s role is checking readiness.','Explain how the same recording instruction has been adapted for a user and a helper.','Use the direct button action in A and readiness checking in B as evidence.')
pair('Bedtime listeners: Little Boat bobbed once, then curled beneath a blanket of stars.','Older story readers: Beneath the glittering sky, the little boat rocked gently towards the sheltered bay.','What do both passages offer despite their different wording?','A gentle imagined scene for readers to enjoy.', ['A complete boating-safety checklist.','An advertisement for a specific boat shop.','A timetable of real ferry departures.'],'Both create a calm narrative picture; one personifies the boat more playfully.','Discuss how “curled beneath a blanket” may suit a bedtime reading, compared with the more detailed setting in B.','Connect the cosy blanket image with settling/rest; note the sky/bay setting in B. Accept personal responses with word evidence.')
pair('Children: Put your finished collage on the drying rack.','Classroom helpers: Leave the drying rack clear so children can put their finished collages there.','What common goal do the instructions serve?','Providing a place for collages to dry.', ['Moving every collage to the bin.','Explaining a fictional collage adventure.','Asking all helpers to make the children’s work.'],'The children use the space and helpers keep it available.','Explain why “leave the rack clear” is useful for helpers, even though it does not tell them to make a collage.','Helpers prepare the shared space; their different role still supports the same drying goal.')
pair('New library members: You may borrow two books. Bring them back next week.','Library desk helpers: Remind new members of the two-book limit and next-week return.','Which rewrite would contradict both texts?','New members may take four books and keep them all term.', ['New members can take two books.','Helpers should explain the return time.','Books are due back next week.'],'Changing the limit and due period alters the policy rather than adapting it for an audience.','Discuss the difference between changing the wording for readers and changing the information itself.','Use the two-book limit and next-week return as facts to preserve; roles/wording can vary.')
pair('To our class: Please lend spare craft sticks for our bridge models. Every extra stick helps a team build.','To local craft shops: Could you donate spare craft sticks to our class bridge project?','How is the same project request adjusted?','A asks classmates to lend; B asks shops to donate supplies.', ['A asks shops to become students.','B changes the project to building a real motorway.','Both claim the bridges are already complete.'],'The supply aim remains while the audience and requested contribution differ.','Explain why the writer identifies the class project when addressing local shops.','Shops need context about who is requesting supplies and what they will support; classmates may already know the project.')
pair('For beginning readers: Dot the dog digs. Dot finds a big red box.','For fluent readers: Dot unearthed a red box and pawed at its rusted clasp.','What is the main audience adaptation?','Vocabulary and sentence detail change while the discovery stays.', ['The dog changes into a bird.','A becomes a factual report about every dog.','B removes the discovery entirely.'],'Both narrate a dog finding a box; B uses unearthed, pawed and clasp with more detail.','Compare one language feature a beginner may find easier in A with one feature adding detail in B.','Accept repetition/short familiar words in A and more specific vocabulary in B; do not claim longer always means better.')
pair('Young visitors: Look for the big blue fish on the aquarium sign. It shows the way in.','Adult visitors: The aquarium entrance is marked by a blue-fish sign beside the east gate.','Why can these both help people find the entrance?','They identify the same sign in different ways.', ['They send visitors to different buildings.','They both require knowledge of every compass direction.','They ask visitors to paint the sign.'],'A emphasises a recognisable picture; B also gives the gate location.','Discuss why the picture cue may help a younger visitor while the gate detail helps someone using directions.','Refer to blue fish versus east gate; do not assume every child or adult uses directions in the same way.')
pair('Team members: Bring a light and a dark shirt so we can make two groups.','Families: Please help players bring one light shirt and one dark shirt for grouping at training.','What should not change when this message is adapted?','Each player needs both a light and a dark shirt.', ['Only adults need to bring a shirt.','Players need two dark shirts instead.','The shirts are for a story performance.'],'Both explain the same equipment need, with different responsibility wording.','Explain how naming the reason for the shirts can help both audiences prepare.','Grouping at training explains the two colours and can reduce confusion; no extra uniform rules are implied.')
pair('Class: Vote for a book swap! We could discover stories without buying new books.','School leaders: Please consider a supervised book swap to give students access to different stories.','Which shared purpose is evident?','Persuading readers to support a book swap.', ['Reporting completed book-swap totals.','Giving instructions for printing books.','Telling an imagined story about school leaders.'],'Vote for and please consider are appeals supporting the same proposed activity.','Compare why “vote” addresses classmates while “consider a supervised” addresses school leaders.','Different audiences have different participation/decision roles; both messages promote the swap.')
pair('First-time visitors: Put your bag in a locker before entering the pool area.','Regular swimmers: Our locker rule remains the same: bags stay outside the pool area.','How do the introductions differ?','One gives the rule directly; the other reminds readers of a familiar rule.', ['One permits bags in the pool and the other forbids them.','Both announce that lockers have closed.','Both give a swimming-race result.'],'The practical purpose and bag location agree, but the second assumes previous familiarity.','Discuss how a reminder differs from a first explanation without changing the rule.','A reminder can refer to what readers have met before; both must still communicate the same requirement.')
pair('Small children listening: The cake grew legs. It ran! It hid! Where could it be?','Independent readers: The runaway cake left a trail of crumbs through the hallway, giving the hungry detective her first clue.','What stays similar in the purpose of these texts?','Both entertain with an impossible runaway cake.', ['Both explain an ordinary baking method.','Both persuade readers to open a real bakery.','Both report verified kitchen experiments.'],'Different language and plot detail serve different readers of the same playful premise.','Explain how the repeated short actions in A and the clue in B invite different kinds of engagement.','A offers simple action/anticipation for listeners; B adds a mystery clue for following an investigation. Avoid fixed age claims beyond the labels.')
pair('Children: Return borrowed pencils to this tray.','Helpers: Keep this tray by the door so children can return borrowed pencils easily.','How do the readers contribute differently to the same goal?','Children return pencils; helpers keep the return place available.', ['Children hide the tray; helpers buy every pencil.','Both are told to take pencils home.','The goal changes from pencils to library books.'],'The shared goal is organised returns, adapted to user and helper responsibilities.','Describe what might be missing if helpers received only the children’s message.','They might not learn their setup responsibility of keeping the tray by the door.')
pair('For new members: Our garden club meets on Mondays after lunch. Come to the small courtyard.','For returning members: Monday meetings are still after lunch in the small courtyard.','Why does B use “still”?','It reassures familiar members that the arrangement has not changed.', ['It means the meeting is silent.','It means the club has stopped meeting.','It changes Mondays to Tuesdays.'],'Still refers to continuity for readers who already know the arrangement.','Explain how the two versions keep the practical information consistent while acknowledging different prior knowledge.','Both preserve Monday, after lunch and the courtyard; B acknowledges an existing routine.')
pair('To classmates: Help make our display welcoming—add a label visitors can understand.','To families: Encourage your child to explain their display label aloud before bringing it in.','Which purpose connects the requests?','Helping visitors understand the display.', ['Making every label use secret codes.','Asking families to build the entire display alone.','Reporting visitors’ opinions after the event.'],'Writing an understandable label and practising its explanation both support clear communication.','Discuss why an adult might be asked to listen to a label rather than write it for the child.','The child remains the author; explaining aloud can reveal unclear wording while the adult supports understanding.')
# Test pairs: new contexts and language.
pair('New bus passengers: Show your ticket to the driver when you board.','Bus drivers: Check each passenger’s ticket as they board.','How do these serve the same process?','They describe the passenger and driver sides of ticket checking.', ['They ask both readers to drive the bus.','They ask passengers to print tickets for everyone.','They describe two unrelated journeys.'],'One presents the ticket and the other checks it; the boarding process is shared.','Explain why the action word needs to change with the reader’s role.','Show fits the ticket holder; check fits the driver. Keep the boarding context in the explanation.')
pair('Young readers: A shadow is a dark shape where something blocks light.','Science-club readers: The diagram shows how an object blocks light and creates a shadow behind it.','Which concept appears in both?','An object blocking light produces a shadow.', ['A shadow gives off its own light.','Every dark shape is a living character.','The texts ask readers to buy a lamp.'],'A gives a basic definition; B refers readers to a representation of the same relationship.','Compare the support a simple definition and a diagram reference give these readers.','The definition introduces meaning; the diagram can locate the parts of the relationship. Do not claim an unseen diagram contains extra features.')
pair('Classmates: Support a quiet lunch table so anyone who needs a calm break has a place to sit.','Principal: Please trial a quiet lunch table for students who need a calm break.','What changes while the proposal stays the same?','Classmates are asked for support; the principal is asked to trial it.', ['The quiet table becomes a noisy music stage.','The intended benefit changes to winning a race.','The first message reports a finished trial.'],'The same calm-space benefit is presented to supporters and a decision-maker.','Explain why the writer should preserve the calm-break reason in both messages.','It explains the shared need to both audiences even though their possible actions differ.')
pair('Bedtime listeners: Little Star yawned. The night tucked it in.','Older story readers: A tired star faded behind the clouds while the valley settled into silence.','Which comparison fits their likely effect?','Both offer a quiet scene, with simpler person-like actions in A and more setting detail in B.', ['Both give exact astronomy measurements.','A is a sales notice and B is a recipe.','Only B has any connection with night.'],'The calm purpose is shared, with different vocabulary and detail for readers/listeners.','Discuss a word or phrase from each text that supports a restful reading.','Accept yawned/tucked it in and tired/settled into silence. Reactions may differ when grounded in the words.')
pair('Exhibitors: Put your name beside your model before the display opens.','Display organisers: Check that every model has its maker’s name before opening.','Why are the two instructions different?','Makers label their own work; organisers check the whole display.', ['Organisers are asked to remove all names.','Makers must label other people’s work with their own names.','The opening time changes between the texts.'],'Both support identifying the makers, adapted to individual and overview responsibilities.','Explain one shared outcome and the different contribution each audience makes.','Every model has the maker’s name; exhibitors add it and organisers check coverage.')
pair('Beginning cooks: Mix the flour and water until no dry flour is left.','Cooking helpers: Show beginners how to stir the flour into the water until the mixture is even.','Which detail is kept consistent?','The ingredients are combined into an evenly mixed result.', ['The helpers replace water with paint.','The beginners must leave all flour dry.','Only helpers are allowed to eat any food.'],'Both concern the same mixing task; one gives a step and one asks for demonstration.','Discuss why a demonstration could help the beginning cook interpret “no dry flour is left”.','It can show the appearance/action associated with the instruction; no new recipe or cooking process is required.')
pair('Young puzzle fans: Come solve our picture hunt! Find every hidden shape.','Families: Encourage children who enjoy visual puzzles to try our picture hunt.','How does the invitation change for the family audience?','It asks adults to encourage participation rather than directly asking them to do the hunt.', ['It changes the picture hunt into a running race.','It reports all children’s completed scores.','It says no child may join.'],'The same activity is promoted through the child’s action or the adult’s supportive role.','Explain which interest the writer appeals to in both versions.','Picture/shape finding and visual puzzles indicate the shared interest; exact skill levels are not stated.')
pair('New school visitors: Ask at the office for a visitor badge before entering classrooms.','Office staff: Give visitors a badge before directing them to classrooms.','Which fact must remain the same across these messages?','The badge comes before entry to the classrooms.', ['Visitors should avoid the office.','Staff should ask visitors to make their own badges.','Badges are collected only after the visit.'],'The order is shared; the visitor requests the badge and office staff provide it.','Discuss how the wording makes the same check-in procedure clear to its two audiences.','Refer to ask versus give/direct and the same before-classrooms sequence.')
assert len(pairs)==32,len(pairs)
models=['The second message is for adults who can help their child pack. The children’s message should ask them to bring their own things.', 'Returning players may already know what a turn is, so a short reminder can be enough. New players need the definition before they can follow the rule.', 'Counting invites younger readers to check the legs themselves. The second text tells readers how to use the labelled diagram for the same information.', 'Both messages describe a comfortable shaded rest place. Classmates can support the idea, while the principal is asked to consider adding the seat.', 'The first version has short sentences and a playful exclamation for listeners. The second uses oversized and adds the stage detail for independent readers.', 'Visitors need to follow the arrows to find the hall. Staff need to put the arrows in place first, so swapping the jobs would be confusing.', 'Watching the helper put in and cover a seed would show what the beginner’s action words mean. Both texts are about the same planting steps.', 'Families need seats to watch, while performers need to be backstage to begin. Both must be ready for two o’clock.', 'The second text seems to expect readers to know what a map is already. It asks them to use a key and compare routes instead of defining a map.', 'Children are invited to join the drawing group and share ideas. Families are asked to encourage their child, not to attend the group themselves.', 'The new user is told which button to press. The helper is asked to check that the learner can find that button before recording.', 'The blanket and curling image sounds cosy for bedtime listeners. The glittering sky and sheltered bay give older readers more setting detail.', 'Children put their finished collages on the rack. Helpers keep that place empty and available so the same drying plan works.', 'The writer can change a direct rule into a reminder for helpers. Two books and next week must stay the same, or the information has changed.', 'The shops may not know the class project, so the request tells them what the sticks are for. Classmates may already know the bridge models.', 'Dot is repeated and the words are short in A. Unearthed and rusted clasp add more specific detail in B.', 'A young visitor can look for the recognisable blue fish. An adult using gate directions gets the extra east-gate detail as well.', 'Knowing the shirts are for making two groups explains why both colours are needed. That helps children and families pack the right items.', 'Classmates can show support by voting. School leaders can consider the proposed supervised activity; both are being persuaded to support a swap.', 'The first version introduces the bag rule directly. The reminder tells regular swimmers it remains the same, without changing where bags must stay.', 'The short ran and hid actions invite listeners to wonder where the cake went. The trail and detective in B add a clue to follow.', 'They might not know to keep the return tray by the door. Returning pencils and arranging an easy return place are different jobs towards one goal.', 'Both versions keep Monday, after lunch and the small courtyard. Still tells existing members that their familiar routine continues.', 'The child can explain what the label means while the adult listens for unclear wording. The adult supports the child’s message instead of replacing it.', 'Passengers hold up their tickets, so show fits them. Drivers inspect the tickets, so check fits their side of boarding.', 'A definition introduces what a shadow is. The second text refers readers to a diagram showing how the object and blocked light relate.', 'Both groups need to understand why the table is proposed. A calm break is the common reason, even though supporting and approving a trial are different actions.', 'Yawned and tucked it in suggest bedtime in A. Tired and settled into silence make B feel quiet too.', 'Each maker adds a name to their own model. Organisers check that no model is missing one, so visitors can identify every maker.', 'The helper can show the stirring and the evenly mixed result. That makes the beginner’s instruction about dry flour easier to recognise.', 'Both appeal to enjoyment of finding pictures and shapes. Families are asked to encourage children with that interest rather than do the hunt themselves.', 'Visitors are told to ask for a badge; staff are told to give one and direct the visitor. Both keep the badge before classroom entry.']
items=[]
for i,(a,b,ask,correct,wrong,why,discuss,note) in enumerate(pairs):
 bank='practice' if i<24 else 'test'; k=i if i<24 else i-24
 context='Text A: '+a+'\nText B: '+b
 def base(n,prompt):return dict(id=f'AC9E3LY01-{bank[0].upper()}-{n:03d}',subject='english',year_level='Year 3',curriculum_code='AC9E3LY01',bank=bank,skill='Similar purposes for different audiences',question=prompt,audio_prompt=prompt,visual={'type':'none'},source={'creator':'SkillrHub original paired texts'})
 pos=k%4;opts=wrong[:];opts.insert(pos,correct);x=base(2*k+1,context+'\n\n'+ask)
 x.update(answers=[{'text':t,'is_correct':j==pos} for j,t in enumerate(opts)],correct_index=pos,explanation={'summary':why,'hint':'Compare what each writer wants the readers to understand or do.'});items.append(x)
 x=base(2*k+2,context+'\n\n'+discuss)
 x.update(grading_mode='adult-review',answers=[],correct_index=None,model_answer=models[i],acceptance_note=note,response_instructions='Discuss the two texts with a partner or adult, or write two sentences. An adult checks that your comparison uses evidence from both texts.',completion_label='I shared my comparison aloud; an adult still needs to check it.',explanation={'summary':why,'hint':'Explain one similarity in purpose and a difference that relates to the readers.'});items.append(x)
Path(__file__).with_name('ac9e3ly01.json').write_text(json.dumps(items,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print('LY01: 32 new pairs, 64 comparison/discussion tasks, 48P/16T.')
